#X text 20 20 ANIM PROBE -- item 77. The Launchpad paces its own flash and pulse animations from incoming MIDI beat clock \, and past an upper and a lower limit it stops tracking and reverts to a default rate. This finds those two limits. Nothing in Cut It can: g_grid lights every pad STATIC \, on channel 1 \, so the animation channels are never exercised by the instrument at all., f 96;
#X text 20 150 ⛔ THE METHOD IS A RULER PAD NEXT TO EACH ANIMATED ONE \, because the eye is bad at estimating a rate and excellent at seeing two things drift apart. Pad 43 is blinked BY THIS PATCH once per beat and pad 44 is FLASHED BY THE DEVICE once per beat \; pad 47 is blinked by this patch once per two beats and pad 48 is PULSED BY THE DEVICE once per two beats. While the device tracks \, each pair holds whatever relative phase it happens to have. The moment it stops \, one pad laps the other -- and there is nothing to judge or remember. 45 and 46 are left dark as a separator., f 96;
#X text 20 330 ⛔ 120 BPM IS THE ONE TEMPO THIS CANNOT MEASURE. 📄 The documented fallback is 120 BPM or the last clock received \, so at 120 a tracking device and a reverted one look identical -- and near it they differ too slowly to see. Judge from well away and sweep TOWARD each limit., f 96;
#X text 700 20 ⚠️ IT ENTERS PROGRAMMER MODE \, AND PROGRAMMER MODE LOCKS OUT THE LAUNCHPAD'S OWN SETTINGS MENU -- so like m_launchpad it hooks r quitting and hands the surface back. ✅ THAT IS ENOUGH TO LEAVE BY \, and the reason is worth knowing: quitting fires on every PATCH LOAD and not only on shutdown \, because /loadPatch runs killpatch.sh first and its first line is oscsend /quitpd. Item 252 \, read off mother.pd and MainMenu.cpp. ⚠️ WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER IS killall pd FROM THE SHELL \, which produces no quitting at all -- run tools/lp-live.sh after one of those., f 96;
#X text 700 200 ⚠️ NOT DEPLOYED WITH Cut It. It is a MENU patch \, so running it costs no killall pd -- which strands the Launchpad in Programmer Mode every time. Copy the folder to /sdcard/Patches/! debug/ and select it., f 96;
#X text 1560 620 KNOB 1 IS THE TEMPO \, AND THE MAP IS EXPONENTIAL RATHER THAN LINEAR. 5 x e^(k x ln 200) puts 5 BPM at one stop and 1000 BPM at the other with EQUAL RELATIVE RESOLUTION EVERYWHERE \, which is what a rate wants -- a linear map over the same span would bury the whole interesting bottom end in the first two degrees of travel. The span is deliberately wider than Cut It's own 10-500: a limit outside the instrument's range is still the answer to item 77., f 62;
#X text 1300 1040 THE PERIOD IS 2500/BPM ms \, and the trigger order is not decoration -- t b f puts the DENOMINATOR in the cold inlet before the 2500 arrives \, because / divides by whatever it was last told. 24 clocks per beat \, so 60000/(24 x BPM)., f 44;
#X text 3400 1660 ⛔ THE ACHIEVED RATE IS THE PROBE CHECKING ITSELF \, and it is the whole reason this readout exists. Count the clock bytes actually emitted in two seconds and multiply by 1.25 -- 96 bytes is 120 BPM to the digit. IF act STOPS FOLLOWING req \, THE LIMIT YOU JUST FOUND IS PD'S AND NOT THE LAUNCHPAD'S. At 1000 BPM metro is running at 2.5 ms and something has to give first \; this says which. Prove the probe before believing the result -- Inquiry Probe's rule \, applied to a rate instead of a reply., f 62;
#X text 20 1950 THE HANDBACK DROPS OWNERSHIP FIRST \, off the leftmost-fires-last trigger \, so no reference blink can land on a surface that is already back in Live Mode. Two writers to one surface is the failure this project bans everywhere else \, and here the second writer would be the device itself., f 50;
#X text 700 1780 KNOB 4 IS THE MODE GESTURE \, and it is a STATE rather than an edge: above 0.9 hand the surface back \, below 0.1 take it. change -1 is what stops a sweep through either rail from re-sending on every value \, and the boot sends it set 1 so a knob 4 already parked at the top can be swept DOWN and back up rather than being stuck. -1 cannot be a real value here \, since mother's knobs are 0 to 1 -- the same reasoning as m_organelle's. IT IS NOT THE WAY OUT \, since r quitting already covers that. It is for reaching the LAUNCHPAD'S OWN SETTINGS MENU mid-session without dropping the probe -- Programmer Mode locks that menu out \, and half of what item 77 might turn up is a device setting., f 50;
#X text 2300 1120 THE CLEAR IS AN until LOOP OVER ALL 99 INDICES \, which m_launchpad deliberately does not have -- there g_grid's first frame IS the clear \, and there is no g_grid here. t b f b f sends status \, index \, velocity in that order because trigger fires RIGHT TO LEFT \, and a note-on assembled in the wrong order is three bytes of nonsense. The increment is the FIRST outlet so the three bytes leave back to back. ⛔ THAT OUTLET IS AN f AND NOT A b \, and the difference is invisible: fed a BANG \, + 1 emits its own stored left value plus one -- so the counter froze at 1 and the loop cleared pad 1 ninety-nine times. Caught by running the probe on the Mac against a fake device rather than by reading it., f 50;
#X text 2800 800 THE FLASH PAD NEEDS BOTH COLOURS AND THE ORDER MATTERS: channel 1 is the colour it alternates FROM \, channel 2 the one it alternates TO \, so 144 goes before 145 off the same trigger. 📄 Palette indices 1 \, 3 and 5 are dark grey \, white and red from the programmer's reference and are NOT verified on this unit -- any two visible colours do this job \, so a wrong index is a wrong colour and never a wrong measurement., f 50;
#X text 20 3070 AUX MARKS AND RESTARTS \, one gesture doing two jobs that belong together. It appends MARK <req> <act> to /sdcard/anim-probe.log so a limit is RECORDED at the moment it is judged \, rather than read off the screen and remembered \, and it sends a MIDI Start -- which is the other half of item 77 \, the reported dip before the device settles onto a new rate. Watch the pads for a second after every press., f 62;
#X text 620 2900 THE LOG APPEND IS u_err's AND Inquiry Probe's \, trap and all. text set wants a LIST and every line arrives with its first word as the SELECTOR \, so list append is load-bearing -- without it Pd answers no method for MARK. text size gives the next line number and text set PAST THE END APPENDS \, which is how the log grows with nothing tracking its length. The file is rewritten 200 ms after every mark rather than at the end \, because a menu patch has no shutdown hook to write it in., f 62;
#X text 3000 1690 ONE midiout \, AND THE PORT IS SET COLD AT LOAD. Everything this probe emits goes through it -- mode SysEx \, the clear loop \, four pad paints \, MIDI clock and Start. Pd 0.49 does not warn about a wrong or missing port at all \, so if nothing lights anywhere \, suspect this and anim-wire.sh before suspecting the device., f 50;
#X text 20 1030 ⚠️ loadbang FIRES BEFORE ALSA CONNECTIONS EXIST \, so the wiring runs at 1500 ms and the first byte to the device at 3500 ms. Anything sent on loadbang itself goes nowhere at all., f 50;
#X text 200 790 THE OLED IS THE WHOLE READOUT. 1 is the title \, 2 the REQUESTED bpm \, 3 the ACHIEVED bpm \, 4 the last EVENT -- wired-N \, prog \, live-k4 \, mark-N -- and 5 the pad legend \, which is the one line you need while your eyes are on the Launchpad rather than the screen., f 50;
